00:03:57.274 --> 00:03:59.588
let's start with your questions.

00:03:59.588 --> 00:04:03.482
Question one,
what made you decide to apply to and

00:04:03.482 --> 00:04:06.466
attend Stanford over other schools?

00:04:09.135 --> 00:04:14.957
&gt;&gt; For me, the first thing that kind
of re-caught my attention was the,

00:04:14.957 --> 00:04:18.225
how dynamic the environment was here.

00:04:18.225 --> 00:04:21.597
Being close to the tech space, but

00:04:21.597 --> 00:04:26.141
the more I started to
know the community here.

00:04:26.141 --> 00:04:30.537
What really made my decision to come
here was just being aware of how

00:04:30.537 --> 00:04:32.670
supportive the community was.

00:04:32.670 --> 00:04:37.094
About how people had done
really interesting things, but

00:04:37.094 --> 00:04:41.527
also not so conventional, or
at least not in my industry.

00:04:41.527 --> 00:04:46.371
And just how welcoming it was
to different perspectives.

00:04:46.371 --> 00:04:46.951
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:04:46.951 --> 00:04:50.285
&gt;&gt; That resonates with me, I was attracted
to Stanford primarily because of

00:04:50.285 --> 00:04:51.910
the Center for Social Innovation.

00:04:51.910 --> 00:04:54.616
I'm hoping to graduate with
a certificate in public management and

00:04:54.616 --> 00:04:55.478
social innovation.

00:04:55.478 --> 00:04:57.093
And there are so many resources and

00:04:57.093 --> 00:04:59.832
courses here that attracted
me to this school initially.

00:04:59.832 --> 00:05:01.506
And then similar to
what Maitane is saying,

00:05:01.506 --> 00:05:04.418
after conversations with friends
who've gone to other business schools.

00:05:04.418 --> 00:05:07.424
The culture of Stanford
really attracted me and

00:05:07.424 --> 00:05:11.348
kind of reminded me of the culture
of Brown, which I enjoyed.

00:05:11.348 --> 00:05:12.950
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I can second that.

00:05:12.950 --> 00:05:14.117
As a joint degree student,

00:05:14.117 --> 00:05:17.280
I was really looking for a community
that would be multi-disciplinary.

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:21.502
And I think Stanford, more than most
other business schools, is exactly that.

00:05:21.502 --> 00:05:25.423
I also wanted to, I don't think I'm
a very entrepreneurial person or

00:05:25.423 --> 00:05:26.429
a big risk taker.

00:05:26.429 --> 00:05:30.036
But I definitely want to be exposed
to those types of individuals and

00:05:30.036 --> 00:05:31.249
those types of ideas.

00:05:31.249 --> 00:05:34.030
And I've been here for
about six months now and

00:05:34.030 --> 00:05:38.107
I already feel as if I'm thinking
a little bit more outside the box.

00:05:38.107 --> 00:05:40.817
I'm collaborating across
different disciplines.

00:05:40.817 --> 00:05:44.724
And again, like Maitane said,
the community is amazing and

00:05:44.724 --> 00:05:48.335
extremely supportive
throughout the entire journey.

00:05:48.335 --> 00:05:51.793
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think my points
have kind of been covered.

00:05:51.793 --> 00:05:56.674
But as someone who didn't come from tech,
didn't come from the startup world.

00:05:56.674 --> 00:06:00.801
Grew up on the East Coast, worked in
the Midwest, I was really looking for

00:06:00.801 --> 00:06:03.276
something to push me
out of my comfort zone.

00:06:03.276 --> 00:06:07.810
And that's something that I think has
been fulfilled here and happens over and

00:06:07.810 --> 00:06:08.573
over again.

00:06:08.573 --> 00:06:14.640
Depending on which class I’m in, or
which group I’m with at the moment.

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:19.537
I feel like I’m so
much more up to speed on tech and

00:06:19.537 --> 00:06:24.206
entrepreneurship and
all that kind of stuff.

00:06:24.206 --> 00:06:26.458
&gt;&gt; Awesome, thank you.

00:06:26.458 --> 00:06:30.914
How certain were you on the career
you wanted to pursue prior

00:06:30.914 --> 00:06:32.621
to starting your MBA?

00:06:32.621 --> 00:06:34.729
&gt;&gt; I had no idea, [LAUGH].

00:06:34.729 --> 00:06:37.800
Consulting kind of fell into my lap
naturally, the thing I enjoyed most

00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:40.425
about my last job, it was similar,
it was client services.

00:06:40.425 --> 00:06:43.127
So you're advising people on
communication strategies and

00:06:43.127 --> 00:06:44.825
really mission-critical moments.

00:06:44.825 --> 00:06:49.452
I realized I liked the dynamic environment
and exposure to different industries.

00:06:49.452 --> 00:06:51.408
So consulting seemed a natural fit,

00:06:51.408 --> 00:06:55.329
in terms of getting exposure to more
quantitative analytical problems.

00:06:55.329 --> 00:06:57.659
While still maintaining those
client relationships and

00:06:57.659 --> 00:06:59.170
exposure to different industries.

00:06:59.170 --> 00:07:03.598
Though I know longer term non-profit
social sectors where I'd like to end up.

00:07:03.598 --> 00:07:08.607
&gt;&gt; For me, I came in knowing I
wanted to work in sports long term.

00:07:08.607 --> 00:07:13.043
And that was great because
ahead of time I was looking at,

00:07:13.043 --> 00:07:17.212
which professors do I want
to make connections with?

00:07:17.212 --> 00:07:21.318
Which alumni do I want to
potentially create connections with?

00:07:21.318 --> 00:07:25.106
But as I've been here,
I've been tempted so

00:07:25.106 --> 00:07:28.706
many times to change my path drastically.

00:07:28.706 --> 00:07:31.593
Because you just get exposed to so
many different industries.

00:07:31.593 --> 00:07:39.529
And so many different areas that Stanford
can kind of help you work towards that.

00:07:39.529 --> 00:07:42.249
Even though I was set going in,
I've had so

00:07:42.249 --> 00:07:45.359
many times where I've
been challenged on that.

00:07:45.359 --> 00:07:50.888
And I've thought about so
many different industries and areas.

00:07:50.888 --> 00:07:54.324
&gt;&gt; So like I said,
I'm still figuring that part out, but

00:07:54.324 --> 00:07:59.489
what I've really enjoyed about the process
is that I think a lot of the resources and

00:07:59.489 --> 00:08:04.370
a lot of the mindset at Stanford is to
really work with your type of time, right?

00:08:04.370 --> 00:08:07.978
So people who know exactly
what they want to go into and

00:08:07.978 --> 00:08:13.109
they're super focused, they'll get
the right resources to to get there,

00:08:13.109 --> 00:08:15.937
as efficiently and as right as possible.

00:08:15.937 --> 00:08:20.529
Whereas for other people who
are exploring different locations or

00:08:20.529 --> 00:08:22.251
different industries,

00:08:22.251 --> 00:08:27.585
there's also that other approach to
career management and career finding.

00:08:27.585 --> 00:08:31.235
And so right now I'm just looking at
the different opportunities there is to

00:08:31.235 --> 00:08:34.783
explore locations and industries, and
really making the most out of it.

00:08:34.783 --> 00:08:39.644
because you do have a lot of exposure
to learn about different things here.

00:08:39.644 --> 00:08:41.390
&gt;&gt; I definitely second
what Danny was saying.

00:08:41.390 --> 00:08:44.203
I came in with a pretty clear
mindset of what I wanted to do.

00:08:44.203 --> 00:08:47.662
I had done finance, I had no idea
whether I want do that again,

00:08:47.662 --> 00:08:51.741
I drastically left and went to
the public sector, worked in nonprofit.

00:08:51.741 --> 00:08:54.248
And I found myself wanting to
marry the two in some way, so

00:08:54.248 --> 00:08:56.400
social impact investing
kind of just made sense.

00:08:56.400 --> 00:08:59.587
But as I've been here and as I have
explored different paths, and what I think

00:08:59.587 --> 00:09:02.580
Stanford does a really good job of is
showing you many different paths and

00:09:02.580 --> 00:09:03.758
making them very tangible so

00:09:03.758 --> 00:09:06.973
you kind of know exactly what to do if
you want to get to different directions.

00:09:06.973 --> 00:09:10.784
And you get to talk to a lot of different
people, especially your classmate,s about

00:09:10.784 --> 00:09:14.542
what they did before, so you see yourself
taking kind of the passions that you think

00:09:14.542 --> 00:09:17.030
you had coming in but
finding other places to set them.

00:09:17.030 --> 00:09:19.852
So now I'm still considering
different options.

00:09:19.852 --> 00:09:22.290
In fact [INAUDIBLE] is something
I want to try out, but

00:09:22.290 --> 00:09:24.743
I'm definitely now more
open than I was coming in.

00:09:24.743 --> 00:09:29.696
&gt;&gt; And to that point, talking to
classmates has been probably one

00:09:29.696 --> 00:09:34.182
of the best resources in terms
of figuring out careers and

00:09:34.182 --> 00:09:38.217
figuring out what industries
might suit you best.

00:09:38.217 --> 00:09:42.278
I don't know if you guys feel the same but
I feel like everyone here has been so

00:09:42.278 --> 00:09:45.325
open and so welcoming about
sharing their experience and

00:09:45.325 --> 00:09:49.578
grabbing coffee with you for for 30
minutes to talk about what they've done in

00:09:49.578 --> 00:09:53.223
the past and all that kind of stuff,
which has been really helpful.

00:09:53.223 --> 00:09:54.426
&gt;&gt; Yeah, totally.
&gt;&gt; No, I agree.

00:09:54.426 --> 00:09:58.627
I think what's also interesting is that
you get a lot of people who are super sure

00:09:58.627 --> 00:10:00.382
about what they wanted to do, but

00:10:00.382 --> 00:10:04.584
just going through that process that you
talked about of being exposed to a whole

00:10:04.584 --> 00:10:08.123
range of different things just gets
them to be super sure about it.

00:10:08.123 --> 00:10:10.665
Or it really does spark something new.

00:10:10.665 --> 00:10:12.564
And there's people who've made pivots and

00:10:12.564 --> 00:10:15.959
just changed completely where they thought
they would go into coming in here.

00:10:15.959 --> 00:10:17.025
So I think that's very cool.

00:10:17.025 --> 00:10:17.615
&gt;&gt; Totally.

00:10:17.615 --> 00:10:21.678
&gt;&gt; And to add to that briefly, I think the
coolest part too is that everyone's going

00:10:21.678 --> 00:10:23.314
through the same journey here.

00:10:23.314 --> 00:10:26.017
So maybe in your old jobs you
feel like you were an outlier,

00:10:26.017 --> 00:10:28.899
because maybe you weren't sure if
you fit and you wanted to pivot.

00:10:28.899 --> 00:10:30.697
Maybe you were the only one
that goes to business school.

00:10:30.697 --> 00:10:32.528
But here,
everyone's going through the same journey.

00:10:32.528 --> 00:10:34.893
So it's really cool to kind of go
through that journey with other people.

00:10:34.893 --> 00:10:38.209
So even if you feel like there's never
enough time to go through everything,

00:10:38.209 --> 00:10:41.116
you can see other journeys and
see if they actually fit with you, and

00:10:41.116 --> 00:10:42.508
it's just a great experience.

00:10:42.508 --> 00:10:43.994
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:10:43.994 --> 00:10:44.800
Do you want to add something to this?

00:10:44.800 --> 00:10:48.228
&gt;&gt; One quick thing, a plug for sort of
the institutional resources at Stanford.

00:10:48.228 --> 00:10:51.148
Beyond the Career Management Center,
you also have access to the schools

00:10:51.148 --> 00:10:53.796
across the street, which are the other
grad schools at Stanford.

00:10:53.796 --> 00:10:55.545
So the Design School, for instance,

00:10:55.545 --> 00:10:59.218
can really help shape your thinking around
visualizing the life that you want.

00:10:59.218 --> 00:11:02.933
And I think that's really unique to
Stanford as a Business School student.

00:11:02.933 --> 00:11:06.413
&gt;&gt; Absolutely, I can echo that I've
felt inspired here, and, yeah,

00:11:06.413 --> 00:11:09.833
at times even a bit distracted,
like, maybe I should do that or

00:11:09.833 --> 00:11:13.021
that sounds interesting,
because there's just so much going on.

00:11:13.021 --> 00:11:16.297
And we know that the MBA is
a general degree, right, and

00:11:16.297 --> 00:11:20.902
a degree where leadership skills that
we're learning here are really going to be

00:11:20.902 --> 00:11:23.571
applicable to many different career paths.

00:11:23.571 --> 00:11:24.689
One of our questions is,

00:11:24.689 --> 00:11:27.893
what have you learned about leadership
that you didn't already know?

00:11:27.893 --> 00:11:31.003
So what have you learned here at
Stanford that you didn't already know

00:11:31.003 --> 00:11:33.510
about leadership, specifically?

00:11:33.510 --> 00:11:35.538
&gt;&gt; I'll start again.

00:11:35.538 --> 00:11:40.360
So to give a little background
on kind of a class we all take

00:11:40.360 --> 00:11:43.779
first quarter called Leadership Labs.

00:11:43.779 --> 00:11:46.263
So you work in a small team of five or

00:11:46.263 --> 00:11:51.322
six students that typically come
from a wide variety of backgrounds,

00:11:51.322 --> 00:11:57.089
and you work through different role plays,
and through different exercises.

00:11:57.089 --> 00:11:59.048
You give each other all kinds of feedback.

00:11:59.048 --> 00:12:03.138
You have an MBA two,
who's been trained on leadership and

00:12:03.138 --> 00:12:06.237
these topics kind of overseeing the group.

00:12:06.237 --> 00:12:08.828
And it's really a crash
course on leadership,

00:12:08.828 --> 00:12:12.015
is the way I look at it throughout
the whole first quarter.

00:12:12.015 --> 00:12:17.638
And to answer your question,
I think the one thing I learned was

00:12:17.638 --> 00:12:24.958
how to strategically facilitate bringing
out the best of everyone on your team and

00:12:24.958 --> 00:12:30.369
making sure that one person isn't
taking up too much space or

00:12:30.369 --> 00:12:35.674
one person who may not be as
inclined to interrupt someone or

00:12:35.674 --> 00:12:40.662
speak their mind extremely openly,
how to bring out and

00:12:40.662 --> 00:12:45.436
kind of balance the conversation
to make sure you're

00:12:45.436 --> 00:12:50.893
getting diverse perspectives and
the best out of everyone.

00:12:50.893 --> 00:12:53.730
&gt;&gt; I'm going with Danny saying
in terms of Leadership Labs.

00:12:53.730 --> 00:12:58.557
Those are really inspiring and also
transformative course for me I would say.

00:12:58.557 --> 00:13:01.199
What I took away from it is
that anyone can be a leader.

00:13:01.199 --> 00:13:03.867
You come in and be more introverted,
more extroverted, but

00:13:03.867 --> 00:13:07.582
it really provides people with a platform
to experiment with the different styles,

00:13:07.582 --> 00:13:09.808
which I thought that was
an awesome opportunity.

00:13:09.808 --> 00:13:14.101
And one other thing is we're filmed
a few times in that class, so

00:13:14.101 --> 00:13:19.285
it's very fun and [LAUGH] humbling to
see yourself on screen with classmates.

00:13:19.285 --> 00:13:21.432
So, yeah.
That's probably what I've learned.

00:13:21.432 --> 00:13:24.570
&gt;&gt; Not to continue the love
fest on Lead Labs, but

00:13:24.570 --> 00:13:27.389
I completely echo everything here, so.

00:13:27.389 --> 00:13:31.721
For some context Lead Labs, you provide
feedback with each other, and I found,

00:13:31.721 --> 00:13:35.351
very similar to what Madison said,
that anyone could be a leader and

00:13:35.351 --> 00:13:39.684
I found myself actually pivoting my own
leadership style based on the things that

00:13:39.684 --> 00:13:41.750
I saw in others that I really admired.

00:13:41.750 --> 00:13:45.614
So, for example, there was someone who
was quiet, confident, more laid back,

00:13:45.614 --> 00:13:49.254
I'm more of a lean in, kind of louder
person, and I I found myself really want

00:13:49.254 --> 00:13:52.838
to spend time with this other person to
learn how do they manage the stressful

00:13:52.838 --> 00:13:56.758
environments when they're leading back,
and the cool part was he was like, hey,

00:13:56.758 --> 00:13:59.120
I actually really wanted
to learn how you lean in.

00:13:59.120 --> 00:14:01.806
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And you know just learning how to adapt

00:14:01.806 --> 00:14:06.164
yourself to different situations and
really start to read the rooms and

00:14:06.164 --> 00:14:09.812
understand, okay,
leadership is all about adaptation.

00:14:09.812 --> 00:14:13.587
So, when to be that quiet leader,
when to be that louder leader,

00:14:13.587 --> 00:14:16.347
when to be a listener,
when to be a questioner.

00:14:16.347 --> 00:14:21.043
So, all that kind of adaptation that
kind of comes through Lead Labs was

00:14:21.043 --> 00:14:25.285
a great place to kind of experiment,
for a lack of a better term.

00:14:25.285 --> 00:14:27.286
&gt;&gt; Yeah,
I'm not going to talk about Lead Labs.

00:14:27.286 --> 00:14:31.565
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So, I think we can learn a lot here.

00:14:31.565 --> 00:14:33.119
I mean you learn a lot about yourself.

00:14:33.119 --> 00:14:35.777
I think that's true of a lot of programs.

00:14:35.777 --> 00:14:40.646
But I think what's unique here is
that when we speak about the culture,

00:14:40.646 --> 00:14:45.190
I think it has a lot of emphasis on
not only your self awareness, but

00:14:45.190 --> 00:14:50.322
also in having other people participate
in that transformation, right?

00:14:50.322 --> 00:14:53.118
So when we talk about
how to provide feedback,

00:14:53.118 --> 00:14:57.421
how to help people along their journey,
and how to also bring in your own

00:14:57.421 --> 00:15:01.673
journey to other people,
I think that's what's really important.

00:15:01.673 --> 00:15:05.932
I think Stanford really tries to make you
kind of cross certain boundaries that you

00:15:05.932 --> 00:15:07.351
may have set for yourself,

00:15:07.351 --> 00:15:11.732
like fake boundaries that you're like,
I would never tell this to someone, or

00:15:11.732 --> 00:15:15.337
I would never have this conversation
because it's very difficult.

00:15:15.337 --> 00:15:18.469
And then all of a sudden,
you find yourself in situations

00:15:18.469 --> 00:15:22.708
where you are going to cross that line,
but in a really positive way, right,

00:15:22.708 --> 00:15:26.624
in a positive way that makes you grow and
really makes you work better and

00:15:26.624 --> 00:15:29.451
just be more aware of how
you interact with others.

00:15:29.451 --> 00:15:30.614
So I think, for me,

00:15:30.614 --> 00:15:34.389
that's been the biggest parting
leadership development here.

00:15:34.389 --> 00:15:36.369
&gt;&gt; That's awesome, thanks for sharing.

00:15:36.369 --> 00:15:41.618
And another question we received is
about a best or most memorable course.

00:15:41.618 --> 00:15:44.544
I know you already mentioned
Lead Labs at depths at length.

00:15:44.544 --> 00:15:50.139
So maybe beyond Lead Labs at best or most
memorable class that you've taken so far.

00:15:50.139 --> 00:15:53.686
&gt;&gt; I've really liked formation
of new ventures here.

00:15:53.686 --> 00:15:59.564
So the objective is that you're going to

00:15:59.564 --> 00:16:04.448
Learn about the different ways
that startups underwent this

00:16:04.448 --> 00:16:09.808
whole journey from idea to financing,
to setting up their teams,

00:16:09.808 --> 00:16:15.088
to everything that set them up either for
success or not success.

00:16:15.088 --> 00:16:18.458
And so what the professors do is they
bring in the entrepreneurs, right.

00:16:18.458 --> 00:16:22.562
So the people who actually went
through this will come to class, and

00:16:22.562 --> 00:16:27.039
then they'll talk about how they set
up their teams, what is important?

00:16:27.039 --> 00:16:27.991
What did they learn?

00:16:27.991 --> 00:16:29.730
What were tough moments?

00:16:29.730 --> 00:16:34.307
And then you get to interview, also,
an entrepreneur of your choosing.

00:16:34.307 --> 00:16:36.857
So it's really different format of class.

00:16:36.857 --> 00:16:41.182
And I really enjoy the fact that
the people who actually were in the case

00:16:41.182 --> 00:16:45.374
studies would come to class and
talk to you about their experience.

00:16:45.374 --> 00:16:47.297
&gt;&gt; That's awesome.

00:16:47.297 --> 00:16:50.765
Sort of mirroring that, I took Formation
of Impact Ventures, which is the same

00:16:50.765 --> 00:16:54.053
model, but focused on social impact
organizations, which was amazing.

00:16:54.053 --> 00:16:56.281
So I won't repeat too much,
because it sounds like the same format,

00:16:56.281 --> 00:16:57.114
which was incredible.

00:16:57.114 --> 00:16:58.454
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But

00:16:58.454 --> 00:17:01.162
I really enjoyed Product Launch,
which is the advanced marketing class.

00:17:01.162 --> 00:17:06.009
It was a nice opportunity to apply what
we had learned in the fall quarter to,

00:17:06.009 --> 00:17:11.310
in terms of strategy, financials, etc.,
to figure out how a product goes from,

00:17:11.310 --> 00:17:14.827
again, idea to an actual company,
so I enjoyed that.

00:17:17.779 --> 00:17:19.175
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
So, I would say for me,

00:17:19.175 --> 00:17:21.618
my favorite class was,
so far, Fiscal Policy.

00:17:21.618 --> 00:17:26.378
So as someone who's doing public policy
masters, I have no policy experience, so

00:17:26.378 --> 00:17:29.038
that was actually my
first foray into policy.

00:17:29.038 --> 00:17:31.935
And it's taught by Keith Hennessey,
who was, you know,

00:17:31.935 --> 00:17:36.138
pretty high up in the Bush administration,
has tons of real-life experience, and

00:17:36.138 --> 00:17:40.100
the way he ran the class was almost like
a mock Senate or a mock Congress, where,

00:17:40.100 --> 00:17:43.396
you know, it was a lot of cold calling,
kind of nervous at first.

00:17:43.396 --> 00:17:48.116
But you find yourself speaking,
you know, as if you're a congressman, or

00:17:48.116 --> 00:17:50.499
you're pitching an idea or a policy.

00:17:50.499 --> 00:17:52.885
And you leave class, you say,
wow, I learned a ton.

00:17:52.885 --> 00:17:56.772
And I just think that the way that the
class was set up, and that fact that it

00:17:56.772 --> 00:18:00.979
was not necessarily something that was
business-specific, but kind of out of

00:18:00.979 --> 00:18:04.840
the realm, just made me appreciate
the classmates that I had even more.

00:18:04.840 --> 00:18:08.409
All of the experience we brought in
that had nothing to do with the kind of

00:18:08.409 --> 00:18:11.574
traditional business,
like finance or something like that.

00:18:11.574 --> 00:18:12.281
&gt;&gt; Awesome.

00:18:12.281 --> 00:18:16.408
Yeah, I've heard great things all of those
classes, but haven't taken any of them,

00:18:16.408 --> 00:18:18.684
so I've got to get them on my list for
next year.

00:18:18.684 --> 00:18:21.903
So my favorite class so
far, without a doubt,

00:18:21.903 --> 00:18:24.634
has been the sports management class.

00:18:24.634 --> 00:18:29.305
So it's taught by George Foster,
as well as two practitioners.

00:18:29.305 --> 00:18:35.793
So the way that works is,
we have a regular Stanford professor,

00:18:35.793 --> 00:18:40.611
accompanied with one or
two industry leaders.

00:18:40.611 --> 00:18:45.916
So that will happen in a bunch of classes,
especially in electives,

00:18:45.916 --> 00:18:52.317
and our two practitioners were Dave Kaval,
who is the president of the Oakland As,

00:18:52.317 --> 00:18:58.464
and Sam Hinkie, who's former general
manager for the Philadelphia 76ers.

00:18:58.464 --> 00:19:01.176
And what I liked so
much about the class was,

00:19:01.176 --> 00:19:05.647
aside from having these people who
have done so much in the industry, and

00:19:05.647 --> 00:19:09.332
are such big names, was,
it was really guest speaker led.

00:19:09.332 --> 00:19:13.507
So we'd have a different guest speaker or
two every week.

00:19:13.507 --> 00:19:16.455
We had people including the president
of the San Francisco Giants.

00:19:16.455 --> 00:19:21.308
We had leaders from the WNBA,
leaders from, really,

00:19:21.308 --> 00:19:26.596
all the major sports in the US,
and some internationally.

00:19:26.596 --> 00:19:30.207
And the class was almost just
a conversation between the students,

00:19:30.207 --> 00:19:33.131
the professor, the practitioners,
and our guests.

00:19:33.131 --> 00:19:38.308
And it really gave us an inside look
into all these different industries.

00:19:38.308 --> 00:19:43.036
And if you're interested in the sports
industry, it's such a no-brainer class,

00:19:43.036 --> 00:19:47.962
and if you're just interested in sports in
general, it's just a fascinating view into

00:19:47.962 --> 00:19:50.845
the world that you just
typically just don't get.

00:19:50.845 --> 00:19:54.744
&gt;&gt; That's great, yeah, and I'll add that
I think it's funny that all our favorite

00:19:54.744 --> 00:19:57.582
classes are so
tied into things that we're interested in.

00:19:57.582 --> 00:20:00.579
One of my favorite classes that I've
taken so far is called Family Business.

00:20:00.579 --> 00:20:03.743
And it's taught by a GSB grad,
Leo Linbeck,

00:20:03.743 --> 00:20:07.502
who flew out from Texas every
week to teach this to us.

00:20:07.502 --> 00:20:11.850
And what was really interesting for
me was that his perspectives were so

00:20:11.850 --> 00:20:15.847
different, and it's like a different
vantage point from tech.

00:20:15.847 --> 00:20:21.519
And so it was really fun to hear his take
on how you run and grow family businesses.

00:20:21.519 --> 00:20:25.959
So, pivoting a little bit away
from the classroom setting.

00:20:25.959 --> 00:20:30.880
Another question has come in about, beyond
the HBSA, what types of organizations

00:20:30.880 --> 00:20:35.043
and/or resources are available at
Stanford for Hispanic students?

00:20:35.043 --> 00:20:37.454
Let's speak a little of that.

00:20:37.454 --> 00:20:40.629
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so I mean, for me,
there's a center on campus,

00:20:40.629 --> 00:20:42.262
El Centro Chicano y Latino.

00:20:42.262 --> 00:20:46.089
So it's it's for
the general community at large.

00:20:46.089 --> 00:20:48.858
So they have tons of events,
cultural events.

00:20:48.858 --> 00:20:51.612
It's a space that you kind of just use,
go and hang out.

00:20:51.612 --> 00:20:54.337
You'll meet a ton of folks
from other programs.

00:20:54.337 --> 00:20:56.961
So, I mean I think maybe
I met two other MBAs, and

00:20:56.961 --> 00:20:59.147
everyone else was from other programs.

00:20:59.147 --> 00:21:03.190
So it's just another space, and
another avenue to explore, to get to know

00:21:03.190 --> 00:21:07.122
the broader community at Stanford,
the Latino community at Stanford.

00:21:07.122 --> 00:21:11.472
So that that's one thing I would
definitely say was a was a good find, and

00:21:11.472 --> 00:21:13.055
a really cool place to be.

00:21:13.055 --> 00:21:16.000
&gt;&gt; Let's say for my experience,
being from Mexico City,

00:21:16.000 --> 00:21:19.134
is the joint collaborations
that there are between HBSA and

00:21:19.134 --> 00:21:23.583
LASA, which is the Latin American Student
Association, are just a great way of kind

00:21:23.583 --> 00:21:26.656
of understanding those two
different perspectives, and

00:21:26.656 --> 00:21:30.446
they're two communities that
are really supportive with each other.

00:21:30.446 --> 00:21:36.756
So those things, I think, are also great
resources to, you know, make advantage.

00:21:36.756 --> 00:21:40.769
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and then I know HBSA last week
had a mixer bringing together different

00:21:40.769 --> 00:21:43.224
Latino groups from
different grad programs.

00:21:43.224 --> 00:21:44.822
So that was a fun way to meet people, and

00:21:44.822 --> 00:21:47.302
I think we're planning to do
more of those events this year.

00:21:47.302 --> 00:21:49.379
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and I couldn't make it, but

00:21:49.379 --> 00:21:53.681
I know that students from the law
school were there, the medical school.

00:21:53.681 --> 00:21:55.130
And Jerry, you saw something?

00:21:55.130 --> 00:21:55.824
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:21:55.824 --> 00:21:56.599
&gt;&gt; That's awesome.

00:21:56.599 --> 00:21:57.915
I love that.

00:21:57.915 --> 00:21:58.492
Okay.

00:21:58.492 --> 00:22:02.101
Next question is about Stanford, and

00:22:02.101 --> 00:22:07.188
how Stanford helps foster diversity and
inclusion.

00:22:07.188 --> 00:22:10.418
Can you guys speak to that?

00:22:10.418 --> 00:22:13.136
&gt;&gt; Sure.
I mean, so starting, I believe,

00:22:13.136 --> 00:22:18.247
in week zero, there there's a kickoff
event called Engage, which brings

00:22:18.247 --> 00:22:23.620
together practitioners who do diversity
equity and inclusion work outside.

00:22:23.620 --> 00:22:26.841
Outside in the Bay Area, they're
consultants for different companies.

00:22:26.841 --> 00:22:30.711
So that's a day-long presentation,
where you're with your section, and

00:22:30.711 --> 00:22:34.878
you go through different exercises, just
to establish a culture of inclusivity in

00:22:34.878 --> 00:22:38.048
your section, and
then hopefully that extends to your class.

00:22:38.048 --> 00:22:41.647
I'm a member of the Diversity Committee
as part of the Student Association, so

00:22:41.647 --> 00:22:43.864
I have a vantage point on planning for
next year.

00:22:43.864 --> 00:22:46.624
So we're planning to
continue that program, and

00:22:46.624 --> 00:22:48.670
build upon it in some exciting ways.

00:22:48.670 --> 00:22:53.203
Beyond that, there are a number of student
identity groups like HBSA, like LASA,

00:22:53.203 --> 00:22:56.655
like BBSA, which is
the Black Business Student Association.

00:22:56.655 --> 00:23:00.851
So I think that those groups all run
fantastic events that recur throughout

00:23:00.851 --> 00:23:01.455
the year.

00:23:01.455 --> 00:23:04.551
So you get a consistent
feeling of inclusivity and

00:23:04.551 --> 00:23:09.653
belonging throughout your class, but
also between classes and BA1s and BA2s.

00:23:09.653 --> 00:23:14.566
We're also working with the administration
to push different initiatives forward,

00:23:14.566 --> 00:23:19.295
to recruit even more diverse viewpoints
and students to come into future classes.

00:23:19.295 --> 00:23:25.227
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and I would give other push
towards kind of the admissions process.

00:23:25.227 --> 00:23:30.292
I've been blown away by the diversity
of our class, both physical diversity,

00:23:30.292 --> 00:23:34.829
ethnic diversity, diversity of thought,
diversity of background,

00:23:34.829 --> 00:23:38.760
really anything you're interested in,
on the background,

00:23:38.760 --> 00:23:43.674
everything you're interested in,
there's magically someone who has deep

00:23:43.674 --> 00:23:47.709
knowledge in that specific area,
which always blows my mind.

00:23:47.709 --> 00:23:52.560
But I really think that the admissions
team does a fantastic job of bringing

00:23:52.560 --> 00:23:58.146
in people from all over the world,
with all different kinds of experiences.

00:23:58.146 --> 00:24:02.165
All different kinds of strengths,
weaknesses, and

00:24:02.165 --> 00:24:04.585
career plans and aspirations.

00:24:04.585 --> 00:24:10.759
And I thinks that makes for
really strong group dynamics and

00:24:10.759 --> 00:24:15.452
kind of a safe space to
kind of be different and

00:24:15.452 --> 00:24:20.283
have different ideas, different thoughts.

00:24:20.283 --> 00:24:22.892
&gt;&gt; Just to add on that.

00:24:22.892 --> 00:24:26.116
I also think that if you find that
the group an identity group or

00:24:26.116 --> 00:24:29.278
something doesn't exist on campus,
you can create it, and

00:24:29.278 --> 00:24:31.643
you'll find the people
that fit that group.

00:24:31.643 --> 00:24:35.519
So I'm working with some students here
to start the first generation and

00:24:35.519 --> 00:24:36.544
low income group.

00:24:36.544 --> 00:24:38.112
We haven't had one before.

00:24:38.112 --> 00:24:40.521
We weren't sure exactly
what the interest would be.

00:24:40.521 --> 00:24:43.963
We send out a email,
we got about 50 responses back.

00:24:43.963 --> 00:24:45.247
And-
&gt;&gt; Over 100.

00:24:45.247 --> 00:24:46.361
&gt;&gt; Over 100 now.

00:24:46.361 --> 00:24:46.949
Yeah, there it is.

00:24:46.949 --> 00:24:51.247
So over 100 now, of people who
were either identify as such or

00:24:51.247 --> 00:24:53.805
want to be advocates of such a program.

00:24:53.805 --> 00:24:58.488
So just the fact that we were able
to do that, just put it in place,

00:24:58.488 --> 00:25:03.526
it was a fairly smooth transition
between us and the administration.

00:25:03.526 --> 00:25:07.949
And just as again,
diversity continues to change and evolve,

00:25:07.949 --> 00:25:12.966
I think you'll be able to find a place
on campus to create what you want.

00:25:12.966 --> 00:25:13.902
&gt;&gt; I see.

00:25:13.902 --> 00:25:18.942
I think what I would add is, just outside
the formal structures of just like

00:25:18.942 --> 00:25:23.822
clubs and societies and associations,
I think there's a real interest

00:25:23.822 --> 00:25:28.227
from people who just to understand
other perspectives, right?

00:25:28.227 --> 00:25:32.473
So, for example, I think I see
here more than in other schools or

00:25:32.473 --> 00:25:35.554
perhaps just in other
experiences I've had.

00:25:35.554 --> 00:25:38.120
Like the people will really go above and

00:25:38.120 --> 00:25:41.320
beyond to understand
a different perspective.

00:25:41.320 --> 00:25:43.024
So they'll be like, okay,

00:25:43.024 --> 00:25:47.639
let's organize small group dinners to
talk about this particular topic that

00:25:47.639 --> 00:25:51.556
perhaps we're not really digging
deep enough to understand it.

00:25:51.556 --> 00:25:56.127
Or let's organize ask me anythings,
or let's do x type of event.

00:25:56.127 --> 00:25:59.787
Or can you share with us what it's
like to work in this country or

00:25:59.787 --> 00:26:01.043
this other country?

00:26:01.043 --> 00:26:06.307
And I think that interest and
how curious and how open the community is,

00:26:06.307 --> 00:26:12.100
and how much it translates that curiosity
into something tangible that people

00:26:12.100 --> 00:26:17.190
can take away with them really helps
with diversity and inclusion and

00:26:17.190 --> 00:26:22.223
just bringing all these things
together into your MBA experience.

00:26:22.223 --> 00:26:24.776
&gt;&gt; Absolutely.
And can you just expand on what ask me

00:26:24.776 --> 00:26:27.831
anythings are for our audience,
that may not know?

00:26:27.831 --> 00:26:31.303
&gt;&gt; Sure.
&gt;&gt; So it is like it's an informal session

00:26:31.303 --> 00:26:38.282
where somebody will volunteer and you can
ask them anything for about nine minutes.

00:26:38.282 --> 00:26:41.154
And so it's just an opportunity
to get to know people better.

00:26:41.154 --> 00:26:45.616
Like I think I've been asked really
interesting questions from my background,

00:26:45.616 --> 00:26:47.202
from places I've lived in.

00:26:47.202 --> 00:26:51.618
And I've also unexpectedly found out
something really random about a classmate.

00:26:51.618 --> 00:26:54.273
For example, one of my classmates,

00:26:54.273 --> 00:26:58.566
it turns out he used to play
classic music professionally.

00:26:58.566 --> 00:27:01.189
And we had no clue he was a musician.

00:27:01.189 --> 00:27:03.400
So you will always find out
something really interesting and

00:27:03.400 --> 00:27:04.405
really fun about someone.

00:27:04.405 --> 00:27:09.307
But also you can really understand
a perspective when it come to

00:27:09.307 --> 00:27:11.259
diversity a lot better.

00:27:11.259 --> 00:27:13.608
&gt;&gt; Absolutely.
And one of the favorite questions for

00:27:13.608 --> 00:27:16.540
AMAs is always,
what matters most to you and why, right?

00:27:16.540 --> 00:27:18.158
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE] unfamiliar.

00:27:18.158 --> 00:27:23.359
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Okay, another question that's
come in, and kind of going off of what

00:27:23.359 --> 00:27:28.903
you were saying, David, was about any of
us being first time MBAs in our family.

00:27:28.903 --> 00:27:34.028
And if so, how did you navigate some of
the challenges of the application and

00:27:34.028 --> 00:27:35.561
internship process?

00:27:35.561 --> 00:27:40.855
So, maybe by a show of hands,
who's a first time MBA in your family?

00:27:40.855 --> 00:27:46.327
Okay, great, so can you speak to
little bit of the challenges and

00:27:46.327 --> 00:27:48.226
how we overcame them?

00:27:48.226 --> 00:27:52.356
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so I can start, so I think for
me, navigating these waters for

00:27:52.356 --> 00:27:55.794
the first time,
you deal with a lot of different things.

00:27:55.794 --> 00:27:58.304
One, you may not understand
how the process works.

00:27:58.304 --> 00:27:59.870
Another part is self-doubt,

00:27:59.870 --> 00:28:03.974
and really not sure where you're going to
fit into the pile of amazing applicants.

00:28:03.974 --> 00:28:09.082
&gt;&gt; And what I did is, first thing I
think is important is to understand

00:28:09.082 --> 00:28:14.023
that being vulnerable throughout
the entire process is power.

00:28:14.023 --> 00:28:18.464
So whether it be reaching out to alumni
and asking questions or putting yourself

00:28:18.464 --> 00:28:22.306
out there at an admissions event to
talk to the admissions officer and

00:28:22.306 --> 00:28:26.372
ask the questions that you really,
really need to understand and know.

00:28:26.372 --> 00:28:30.886
Going in and breaking down your own
narrative and owning that narrative.

00:28:30.886 --> 00:28:35.422
I think are all part of the application
process that truly allowed me to grow

00:28:35.422 --> 00:28:38.086
into not just being
comfortable applying or

00:28:38.086 --> 00:28:42.838
going through the challenge, but also
just kind of saying, owning myself and

00:28:42.838 --> 00:28:47.094
my story, regardless of whether I was
going to get into Stanford or not.

00:28:47.094 --> 00:28:50.291
So I think that was really big for me.

00:28:50.291 --> 00:28:53.015
I also had a little bit of fun with it,
right?

00:28:53.015 --> 00:28:55.719
So my family didn't know what an MBA was.

00:28:55.719 --> 00:28:59.470
So I had fun educating them and
kind of like walking them the process and

00:28:59.470 --> 00:29:02.394
I brought them into kind of
the application process and

00:29:02.394 --> 00:29:06.353
having that support even if it's not,
when I applied to an MBA I did this.

00:29:06.353 --> 00:29:08.343
But just saying hey,
what do you think about this story?

00:29:08.343 --> 00:29:09.116
You remember when this happened?

00:29:09.116 --> 00:29:10.860
And we would kind of reminisce, and

00:29:10.860 --> 00:29:13.574
really help me think through
my narrative and story.

00:29:13.574 --> 00:29:17.040
So I think even if your family or the
people around you are not quite familiar

00:29:17.040 --> 00:29:18.867
with it, bring them into the process.

00:29:18.867 --> 00:29:23.212
Because in the end of the day, it's about
owning your narrative and your story.

00:29:23.212 --> 00:29:26.288
&gt;&gt; That was really well put,
hard to follow.

00:29:26.288 --> 00:29:30.784
I think I would echo what David just said,
but I really emphasize,

00:29:30.784 --> 00:29:35.441
don't be shy about turning to mentors and
seeking those people out,

00:29:35.441 --> 00:29:37.625
whether or not they have an MBA.

00:29:37.625 --> 00:29:41.261
I had to explain the decision to my family
and they didn't understand like but

00:29:41.261 --> 00:29:44.230
you went to a good undergrad program,
why do you need this degree?

00:29:44.230 --> 00:29:47.577
And I realized I wasn't happy
in the career I had chosen and

00:29:47.577 --> 00:29:51.877
that I needed an MBA to pivot to things
that I cared or rather I wanted an MBA in

00:29:51.877 --> 00:29:56.679
order to effect change at a greater scale
and towards something that I cared about.

00:29:56.679 --> 00:30:00.166
So I really relied on my mentors
that I had cultivated in both

00:30:00.166 --> 00:30:03.741
the office setting but
also from years before in undergrad.

00:30:03.741 --> 00:30:06.948
And they were really helpful in
terms of navigating the process, and

00:30:06.948 --> 00:30:09.505
again helping me flesh out my story,
like David said.

00:30:09.505 --> 00:30:13.865
And not being shy about being vulnerable
and honest about the experiences that

00:30:13.865 --> 00:30:17.382
had shaped me and why I thought I
would be a good candidate here.

00:30:17.382 --> 00:30:21.893
&gt;&gt; I think another thing to look into is
that there are programs out there that

00:30:21.893 --> 00:30:26.274
help, specifically, Black and
Hispanic potential MBA applicants.

00:30:26.274 --> 00:30:29.659
Like MLT, Management Leaders for Tomorrow.

00:30:29.659 --> 00:30:33.538
So to David's point and
to Madison's point,

00:30:33.538 --> 00:30:38.025
seek out informal mentors,
put yourself out there.

00:30:38.025 --> 00:30:39.477
And really look for

00:30:39.477 --> 00:30:44.743
ways that you can become more informed
on the MBA application process

00:30:44.743 --> 00:30:50.217
to give yourself kind of the opportunity
to put your best foot forward.

00:30:50.217 --> 00:30:53.999
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and I don't want to speak for
the group but folks who are watching,

00:30:53.999 --> 00:30:57.482
I'd be happy to talk to anyone
who's watching who has questions.

00:30:57.482 --> 00:30:58.237
&gt;&gt; Definitely.

00:30:58.237 --> 00:30:58.865
&gt;&gt; Same.

00:30:58.865 --> 00:31:00.751
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; You can always get in touch with us.

00:31:00.751 --> 00:31:05.944
And we'll have more information about how
to get in touch with us towards the end.

00:31:05.944 --> 00:31:08.802
Okay, so
first in our families to get MBAs,

00:31:08.802 --> 00:31:12.993
navigating which programs to choose,
we're now at Stanford.

00:31:12.993 --> 00:31:16.085
What's been most surprising
about this experience?

00:31:16.085 --> 00:31:17.645
Was there anything unexpected?

00:31:19.989 --> 00:31:24.349
&gt;&gt; I think the degree of personal
change that the program ignites.

00:31:24.349 --> 00:31:29.279
[LAUGH] We all go in here wanting to
refine and improve our soft skills,

00:31:29.279 --> 00:31:32.605
leadership skills, and
you expect all that.

00:31:32.605 --> 00:31:37.130
But I don't think that you really
understand how much you're

00:31:37.130 --> 00:31:41.393
putting yourself up to expose
yourself to really question

00:31:41.393 --> 00:31:46.281
perhaps fundamental things about
yourself or about your career.

00:31:46.281 --> 00:31:50.016
So it may be like what we spoke about,
that you may just have a complete,

00:31:50.016 --> 00:31:52.341
180 change of what you want to do in life.

00:31:52.341 --> 00:31:55.744
But it may also be things like
you may understand something

00:31:55.744 --> 00:32:00.366
about yourself through this whole like
leadership development or through other

00:32:00.366 --> 00:32:04.138
Interactions that you didn't know or
you hadn't thought about it before, or

00:32:04.138 --> 00:32:06.724
you hadn't recognized what
impact it has on others.

00:32:06.724 --> 00:32:11.571
And so that really feels like this
motivation and this passion to grow and

00:32:11.571 --> 00:32:13.243
to challenge yourself.

00:32:13.243 --> 00:32:17.620
But sometimes it's tough because you're
going to have to make difficult decisions

00:32:17.620 --> 00:32:20.611
about your career and
your personal life and yourself.

00:32:20.611 --> 00:32:22.575
But I think that's what's great.

00:32:22.575 --> 00:32:27.485
It was a surprise that it would be
such a big scale like change perhaps,

00:32:27.485 --> 00:32:30.410
but it's something really satisfying.

00:32:33.196 --> 00:32:37.222
&gt;&gt; So
I would echo a lot of what she just said.

00:32:37.222 --> 00:32:40.007
Like I said,
I believe that vunerability is power,

00:32:40.007 --> 00:32:42.235
that's why I was attracted to Stanford.

00:32:42.235 --> 00:32:45.516
I was not prepared for
how much that was the case.

00:32:45.516 --> 00:32:48.256
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; So, we discuss AMAs, there's also

00:32:48.256 --> 00:32:53.294
a talk series where one of our classmates
will go up and share their narrative.

00:32:53.294 --> 00:32:58.057
Even in Lee Labs, I think all of
us cried in Lee Labs at one point.

00:32:58.057 --> 00:32:59.724
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; It's touchy feely,

00:32:59.724 --> 00:33:01.053
which is a class I haven't taken yet.

00:33:01.053 --> 00:33:02.620
Apparently, everyone cries so
here we go again.

00:33:02.620 --> 00:33:03.669
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:33:03.669 --> 00:33:06.211
&gt;&gt; And I just think that's really unique

00:33:06.211 --> 00:33:07.170
to Stanford.

00:33:07.170 --> 00:33:10.606
I don't know many other, I have a lot of
friends that are in other MBA programs,

00:33:10.606 --> 00:33:12.371
I don't think that's always the case.

00:33:12.371 --> 00:33:16.669
And one thing I would also add, too,
is how much resources are available here.

00:33:16.669 --> 00:33:21.461
I think you also have an idea that an MBA
program's going to have a lot of great

00:33:21.461 --> 00:33:22.323
resources.

00:33:22.323 --> 00:33:26.831
But if I think of something it exists,
and it's something that's valuable, and

00:33:26.831 --> 00:33:28.213
I can take advantage of.

00:33:28.213 --> 00:33:33.148
So I think allowing yourself to be open
to everything and to really look for

00:33:33.148 --> 00:33:36.026
the resources that are out there already.

00:33:36.026 --> 00:33:37.741
I mean, there's just so

00:33:37.741 --> 00:33:42.896
much to take advantage of that that was
one of the biggest surprises for me.

00:33:44.645 --> 00:33:46.574
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:33:46.574 --> 00:33:49.920
I've been surprised by how much learning
takes place outside of the classroom.

00:33:49.920 --> 00:33:53.808
I think we said that a lot in the
beginning, but our classmates have really

00:33:53.808 --> 00:33:58.215
been such a source of insight, different
perspective, out of our comfort zones.

00:33:58.215 --> 00:34:01.912
Oftentimes I've found myself
at a small group dinner and

00:34:01.912 --> 00:34:04.770
am like, I'm the only American here.

00:34:04.770 --> 00:34:07.101
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And just the learning and

00:34:07.101 --> 00:34:11.702
the openness that people bring
to the table has shocked me.

00:34:14.454 --> 00:34:18.380
&gt;&gt; I've also been really
surprised at how busy we've been.

00:34:18.380 --> 00:34:18.979
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

00:34:18.979 --> 00:34:23.602
just how much we're constantly
forced to make trade offs.

00:34:23.602 --> 00:34:26.252
We talked about the mixer that
HPSA hosted the other night, and

00:34:26.252 --> 00:34:29.819
they couldn't make it because there were
literally three other things happening.

00:34:29.819 --> 00:34:34.982
And there was this wonderful screening
taking place, a film, a documentary

00:34:34.982 --> 00:34:40.475
called, Groomed, that a GSB alumni put
together, and I just couldn't miss it.

00:34:40.475 --> 00:34:45.757
But I think I've been really surprised
that the amount, the optionality, I guess.

00:34:45.757 --> 00:34:52.611
And all the things happening, and really
the reality that we can't take it all in.

00:34:52.611 --> 00:34:57.489
And I'm reminded of how Disneyland
wasn't designed to allow you to go

00:34:57.489 --> 00:35:01.799
on every ride in one day or
even a week, and the GSB is that way.

00:35:01.799 --> 00:35:05.987
We have two short years here,
and it's really not designed for

00:35:05.987 --> 00:35:09.069
you to take every class and
do everything, but

00:35:09.069 --> 00:35:13.432
to make your own, pick what you
want from the buffet kind of thing.

00:35:13.432 --> 00:35:17.768
&gt;&gt; You guys completely covered
everything [LAUGH] I had thought of.

00:35:17.768 --> 00:35:22.668
&gt;&gt; Yeah, cool, so
we have a career question.

00:35:22.668 --> 00:35:26.841
How does the campus recruiting
process work at the GSB, and

00:35:26.841 --> 00:35:30.108
what types of resources
does Stanford offer?

00:35:30.108 --> 00:35:30.838
I can give you this one off.

00:35:30.838 --> 00:35:35.476
So there's a few different
types of processes.

00:35:35.476 --> 00:35:38.786
So one, there are a set of companies and

00:35:38.786 --> 00:35:44.018
a set of industries that will
do formal on campus recruiting.

00:35:44.018 --> 00:35:49.682
They'll have info sessions
around November or December.

00:35:49.682 --> 00:35:55.208
They have formal application processes and
interview processes that they've been

00:35:55.208 --> 00:36:00.666
doing, and coordinating with Stanford
career team for years and years and years.

00:36:00.666 --> 00:36:04.386
That's kind of the stuff like
consulting and investment banking.

00:36:04.386 --> 00:36:09.412
Some of the tech companies,
and then aside from that,

00:36:09.412 --> 00:36:16.640
we have one of the best resources is just
the alumni, so I've been blown away.

00:36:16.640 --> 00:36:21.620
I guess one of the things that surprised
me most is how helpful the alumni been,

00:36:21.620 --> 00:36:26.524
and how responsive they've been when I
just cold reach out via email to an alum

00:36:26.524 --> 00:36:29.638
that's in an industry that's relevant for
me.

00:36:29.638 --> 00:36:31.366
So that's been a great source.

00:36:31.366 --> 00:36:36.383
There's also a never-ending list
of internships, and so we're all

00:36:36.383 --> 00:36:42.173
first year so we're more experienced
with the internship recruiting process.

00:36:42.173 --> 00:36:48.546
But a never-ending list of internships
posted that scroll through, and

00:36:48.546 --> 00:36:53.786
have alerts set up to forward
me stuff that's in a location or

00:36:53.786 --> 00:36:56.478
industry I'm interested in.

00:36:56.478 --> 00:37:01.318
And then lastly, sorry to take so
many of these, but

00:37:01.318 --> 00:37:04.618
we have a pretty large career team.

00:37:04.618 --> 00:37:09.374
And no matter the industry you're in,
how large or how small,

00:37:09.374 --> 00:37:13.527
there is a career advisor
dedicated to your industry.

00:37:13.527 --> 00:37:18.050
So for sports for example, there's maybe
only a couple people each year that

00:37:18.050 --> 00:37:21.739
are interested in that,
there's actually a career advisor,

00:37:21.739 --> 00:37:24.960
one I met with yesterday,
who specializes in sports.

00:37:24.960 --> 00:37:28.633
So that's been a great resource for
me as well.

00:37:28.633 --> 00:37:33.793
And then on the flip side,
there's also the Stanford career

00:37:33.793 --> 00:37:38.953
team that works on reaching out
to different companies, and

00:37:38.953 --> 00:37:44.331
establishing formal connections
between them and Stanford.

00:37:44.331 --> 00:37:50.372
And again, there's someone dedicated to
sports, despite how niche it might seem.

00:37:50.372 --> 00:37:54.161
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think that was a very
thorough explanation of the resources.

00:37:54.161 --> 00:37:57.331
&gt;&gt; Sorry.
&gt;&gt; No, it's great.

00:37:57.331 --> 00:38:00.191
One thing I'd add, we have blast email,

00:38:00.191 --> 00:38:04.615
which is sort of a list that
connects all the MBA ones, MBA twos.

00:38:04.615 --> 00:38:07.871
And people will just throw different
opportunities out from their old

00:38:07.871 --> 00:38:11.691
companies, etc., and even networking
with students in our class are MBA twos.

00:38:11.691 --> 00:38:16.472
They'll know someone who knows someone
who's looking for an intern, so

00:38:16.472 --> 00:38:20.943
that's a good resource,
as well as student clubs, I would say, but

00:38:20.943 --> 00:38:23.430
beyond that I think Danny covered it.

00:38:23.430 --> 00:38:27.595
The only thing I would add which is
something that I'm doing this summer,

00:38:27.595 --> 00:38:30.363
is taking advantage of
the global experiences.

00:38:30.363 --> 00:38:36.251
So there's GMIX, which is the opportunity
for you to go and work for a company for

00:38:36.251 --> 00:38:42.063
a minimum of four weeks in a different
country that you never lived or worked in.

00:38:42.063 --> 00:38:47.182
And so, it just gives you the chance
to explore a new location,

00:38:47.182 --> 00:38:52.023
perhaps a new industry,
it's a really easy way to do that.

00:38:52.023 --> 00:38:54.617
And so, there's GMIX,
which is through the GSP.

00:38:54.617 --> 00:38:58.785
There's also kind of broader
Stanford opportunities,

00:38:58.785 --> 00:39:04.078
like through Seed, which is a program
that we have to help entrepreneurs

00:39:04.078 --> 00:39:08.614
in the developing world build
up their management skill set.

00:39:08.614 --> 00:39:10.577
And then towards the end of the program,

00:39:10.577 --> 00:39:14.286
they get access to offer internships
as well, to the Standford community.

00:39:14.286 --> 00:39:19.276
So then that's something that business
students can also tap into if they're

00:39:19.276 --> 00:39:22.163
interested in working in emerging markets.

00:39:22.163 --> 00:39:25.245
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I guess one thing to add, it's
not necessaritly a formal resource, but

00:39:25.245 --> 00:39:28.008
I would say there's a lot of things
that happen here that are informal.

00:39:28.008 --> 00:39:31.047
So the cool thing about going to
Stanford and having a Stanford MBA is

00:39:31.047 --> 00:39:34.723
that the majority of people you reach out
to, whether it's a call or the email, or

00:39:34.723 --> 00:39:37.407
something of that nature,
they'll reach back out to you.

00:39:37.407 --> 00:39:41.990
So I'm going into an industry that there's
not a lot of recruiting that goes on, so

00:39:41.990 --> 00:39:44.288
a lot of it had to be
me hitting the ground.

00:39:44.288 --> 00:39:47.521
And I know it sounds kind if intimidating
at first, but you realize that's kind

00:39:47.521 --> 00:39:50.672
of part of being at Stanford, at being
in the entrepreneurial environment.

00:39:50.672 --> 00:39:54.670
There's also a lot of other classmates
who I think, for a lot of folks coming to

00:39:54.670 --> 00:39:57.886
an MBA, they feel like okay,
an MBA is kind of the means to the end.

00:39:57.886 --> 00:40:00.655
And you realize the NBA is a means and
of itself, and

00:40:00.655 --> 00:40:04.919
folks will come in thinking that they're
ready to hit this recruiting cycle.

00:40:04.919 --> 00:40:07.966
And then, you talk to them now,
they're kind of like, yeah, you know,

00:40:07.966 --> 00:40:11.014
I'm just going to start my own thing or
I'm going to create my own project for

00:40:11.014 --> 00:40:13.643
the summer or I'm going to join
a start-up to try something new.

00:40:13.643 --> 00:40:16.400
So I think that's another
thing that is unspoken,

00:40:16.400 --> 00:40:19.038
but I think a lot of things
happen in that avenue.

00:40:19.038 --> 00:40:23.568
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and to that point,
on the entrepreneurship part, something we

00:40:23.568 --> 00:40:28.624
didn't touch on was, there is a wealth of
resources for people who want to start

00:40:28.624 --> 00:40:33.628
their own companies, or work for small
start ups over the summer or full time.

00:40:33.628 --> 00:40:39.532
So there is certainly no lack of
resources for entrepreneurs as well.

00:40:39.532 --> 00:40:45.614
&gt;&gt; Great, and we're coming towards
the end of our time together today.

00:40:45.614 --> 00:40:49.484
One question that I think would be
great to kind of round this out.

00:40:49.484 --> 00:40:53.469
Knowing what you know now, about Stanford
and the GSB and this experience,

00:40:53.469 --> 00:40:56.705
if you could start all over again,
rewind back to September,

00:40:56.705 --> 00:40:59.085
is there anything you
would do differently?

00:40:59.085 --> 00:41:04.478
Or even maybe rewind back through
the application process, before?

00:41:04.478 --> 00:41:06.953
Yeah, what would you do differently?

00:41:06.953 --> 00:41:10.008
&gt;&gt; I think going back to your
point about the resources and

00:41:10.008 --> 00:41:14.260
an optionality I wish I would have spent
a little bit more time thinking about

00:41:14.260 --> 00:41:17.462
the path that I at least wanted
to start on when I got here.

00:41:17.462 --> 00:41:20.552
I think for the first like month or
two, I came kind of thinking,

00:41:20.552 --> 00:41:22.429
let's be completely open-minded.

00:41:22.429 --> 00:41:23.796
There's too much.

00:41:23.796 --> 00:41:26.109
It's so hard to be completely open-minded,

00:41:26.109 --> 00:41:29.326
and you start to get FOMO because
you're making too many trade offs.

00:41:29.326 --> 00:41:33.697
I think, had I spent maybe a little
bit more time in the front end,

00:41:33.697 --> 00:41:36.402
just exploring what actually existed,

00:41:36.402 --> 00:41:41.908
coming in with some type of plan to then
pivot from would have been really helpful.

00:41:41.908 --> 00:41:46.649
&gt;&gt; I'd second that, but I think I had
a slightly different take, which was,

00:41:46.649 --> 00:41:51.539
I think a lot of people, or the majority
of people here, are used to being able

00:41:51.539 --> 00:41:56.154
to do everything in a very short amount
of time and being very efficient.

00:41:56.154 --> 00:41:59.559
And so, sometimes you're under
the illusion that you will be able to do

00:41:59.559 --> 00:42:01.961
everything once you're here,
and you're not.

00:42:01.961 --> 00:42:06.581
And so I think just taking the time,
I think taking some time, a,

00:42:06.581 --> 00:42:10.949
just to like, shift gears before
coming to business school,

00:42:10.949 --> 00:42:13.648
really getting your priorities set.

00:42:13.648 --> 00:42:16.513
Then you are able to articulate
just what you're working towards or

00:42:16.513 --> 00:42:18.485
what you don't know that
you want to explore.

00:42:18.485 --> 00:42:23.128
It's really important because, yeah,
you won't be able to do everything, and

00:42:23.128 --> 00:42:26.308
just being able to prioritize
makes a huge difference.

00:42:26.308 --> 00:42:30.414
&gt;&gt; Yeah, those are certainly, well, that's
definitely what I would do differently,

00:42:30.414 --> 00:42:31.439
starting at the GSP.

00:42:31.439 --> 00:42:35.776
But looking back to the application
process, I think it would have been more

00:42:35.776 --> 00:42:39.120
useful for me to start the essay
drafting process sooner.

00:42:39.120 --> 00:42:42.454
And really key into what
story I wanted to tell.

00:42:42.454 --> 00:42:45.587
Because I think it's important to
tell a story in your essay and

00:42:45.587 --> 00:42:48.080
that occurred to me a little
later in the process.

00:42:48.080 --> 00:42:51.921
I wish I had brainstormed that
a bit more strategically.

00:42:51.921 --> 00:42:56.732
&gt;&gt; Taking it back to the GSB, one thing
I wished I'd have done differently.

00:42:56.732 --> 00:43:02.924
I think I realized later than I would
have wanted to how open alums and

00:43:02.924 --> 00:43:08.912
professors are to get coffee with you,
to meet up with you, etc.

00:43:08.912 --> 00:43:12.899
And I still have another
year to go to do this, but

00:43:12.899 --> 00:43:18.447
I'm just now starting to have
informal conversations with alums,

00:43:18.447 --> 00:43:23.340
have breakfast and
coffee with professors in my industry.

00:43:23.340 --> 00:43:28.114
And I wish I would have taken
advantage of that from day one,

00:43:28.114 --> 00:43:30.704
as opposed to in quarter three.

00:43:30.704 --> 00:43:32.161
&gt;&gt; That's awesome.

00:43:32.161 --> 00:43:35.122
I think I would echo all of that,
and I'm so grateful for

00:43:35.122 --> 00:43:38.660
the time we got to spend together today,
so thank you for joining.

00:43:38.660 --> 00:43:42.199
And thank you to our audience for
participating in this event and for

00:43:42.199 --> 00:43:43.451
your great questions.

00:43:43.451 --> 00:43:45.845
Thank you for joining us today.

00:43:45.845 --> 00:43:47.061
Have a great day.

00:43:47.061 --> 00:43:52.165
[MUSIC]

